  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A rock from another star accelerated leaving our sun. Spotted on 19 October 2017 by a telescope on Haleakala, in Hawaii.",
        "visual_query": "hawaiian volcano summit observatory",
        "visual_fallback": "observatory dome"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Its path was hyperbolic. Eccentricity 1.2, hitting about 87 kilometres a second at closest approach. Nothing in our solar system can throw a rock like that.",
        "visual_query": "dark asteroid tumbling space",
        "visual_fallback": "space rock"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Comets speed up because ice boils off and shoves them. But this thing had no tail. No coma. Nothing visible leaving it at all.",
        "visual_query": "comet approaching distant sun",
        "visual_fallback": "distant sun"
      },
      {
        "narration": "One Harvard physicist suggested an alien lightsail. Most astronomers went the other way: nitrogen or hydrogen ice, venting invisibly from inside.",
        "visual_query": "starfield night sky telescope",
        "visual_fallback": "star field"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By 2024 fourteen more objects were doing the same thing. It finally had a category: a dark comet, not a spaceship.",
        "visual_query": "deep space star cluster",
        "visual_fallback": "night sky"
      }
    ],
